<PageLayout
  title="Net Worth by Age: 2022 Federal Reserve Data | Cooked Finance"
  description="Median and mean U.S. family net worth by age from the Federal Reserve's 2022 Survey of Consumer Finances, with definitions and careful interpretation."
  path="/benchmarks/net-worth-by-age"
  eyebrow="Federal Reserve benchmark"
  heading="Net worth by age, without the fake precision."
  lead="These are published 2022 Survey of Consumer Finances estimates for broad U.S. family age groups—not a calculator-generated personal percentile."
  updated="August 17, 2026"
>
  <ContentNote title="What this table compares" tone="caution">
    <p>
      The Survey of Consumer Finances reports on <strong>families</strong>, grouped
      here by the age of the family’s reference person. It does not describe an
      individual person of every exact age. Do not compare an individual balance
      with this table as though the populations were identical.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <h2>2022 net worth by age group</h2>
  <p>
    The Federal Reserve defines net worth as a family’s assets minus its
    liabilities. Its 2022 report presents dollar estimates in thousands of
    inflation-adjusted 2022 dollars. The table below converts those published
    values to full dollars without otherwise modifying them.
  </p>

  <EvidenceTable
    caption="Median and mean U.S. family net worth by age of reference person, 2022"
    sourceNote="Source: Federal Reserve, Changes in U.S. Family Finances from 2019 to 2022, table 2. Amounts are 2022 dollars."
  >
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Age of reference person</th>
        <th scope="col">Median net worth</th>
        <th scope="col">Mean net worth</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Under 35</th><td>$39,000</td><td>$183,500</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">35–44</th><td>$135,600</td><td>$549,600</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">45–54</th><td>$247,200</td><td>$975,800</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">55–64</th><td>$364,500</td><td>$1,566,900</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">65–74</th><td>$409,900</td><td>$1,794,600</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">75 or older</th><td>$335,600</td><td>$1,624,100</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>
  <p>
    Primary source:
    <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/october-2023-changes-in-us-family-finances-from-2019-to-2022.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Federal Reserve, “Changes in U.S. Family Finances from 2019 to 2022,” table 2<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
    The report is based principally on final data from 4,602 families interviewed
    for the 2022 SCF and uses survey weights to estimate the population.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the median and mean are so different</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>median</strong> is the middle observation: half of the estimated
    population is below it and half is above it. The <strong>mean</strong> adds
    all net worth and divides by the number of families. A relatively small
    number of very wealthy families can pull the mean far above the median.
  </p>
  <p>
    For personal context, the median is usually less sensitive to very high
    values. The Federal Reserve also cautions that medians are not additive:
    median assets minus median liabilities will not necessarily equal median net
    worth. Neither statistic tells you what one household “should” have.
  </p>

  <h2>What is included in SCF net worth?</h2>
  <p>
    The SCF balance sheet is much broader than cash and retirement accounts. Its
    asset categories include financial assets and nonfinancial assets such as
    vehicles, homes, other residential real estate, nonresidential real estate,
    and privately held businesses. Liabilities include housing debt, installment
    loans, credit-card balances, lines of credit, and other debt categories. See
    the Federal Reserve report’s
    <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/october-2023-changes-in-us-family-finances-from-2019-to-2022.htm#xappendix-b-survey-procedures-and-statistical-measures-e93c647a" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Appendix B definitions<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How to calculate your own net worth</h2>
  <p><strong>Personal net worth = current value of assets − current liabilities.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Be consistent. If you include mortgage debt, also include the current value
    of the home; otherwise the calculation is incomplete. Use balances from the
    same date, avoid counting the same asset twice, and remember that estimated
    sale values can change.
  </p>

  <h2>Four reasons not to turn this into a verdict</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each age band covers people at many different life stages.</li>
    <li>Housing status, pensions, education, family structure, inheritance, disability, and location can create large differences.</li>
    <li>The survey describes 2022; asset prices and individual balances change afterward.</li>
    <li>A net-worth comparison says little about cash flow, liquidity, insurance, goals, or wellbeing.</li>
  </ul>

  <ContentNote title="Why Cooked Finance does not assign an age percentile">
    <p>
      The published table provides a median and mean for six broad age groups.
      It does not provide enough verified information to claim that an arbitrary
      balance is ahead of an exact percentage of same-age people. We would rather
      show a defensible table than manufacture a precise-sounding rank.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

